                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It's a complete ghost town now.\n\nI did really enjoy Brain/Out when I played it years ago. At its core, the game is basically a flat, 2D version of Counter-Strike, but it had some genuinely refreshing ideas that made it stand out.\n\nThe concept of being invisible and only being revealed when you fired a shot or when another player was looking in your direction was incredibly immersive. It created a unique cat-and-mouse dynamic where positioning, awareness, and knowing when to reveal yourself could completely change an encounter. The game also had a vast array of different weapons, and the combination of the weapon variety with the stealth/reveal mechanics made the combat surprisingly fun.\n\nThere were also several different game modes, including Capture the Base, Deathmatch, and others, which gave the game variety. When there were actually enough players around, there was a lot of fun to be had here.\n\nUnfortunately, the player population has declined to the point where the game is basically unsustainable for me now.\n\nThere is simply nobody on most of the time, and when I do find someone, I'm often forced to join their game, which usually means playing against someone from Russia or another distant region with vastly better ping than I have. That's obviously not a great experience for a game where fast reactions and accurate shooting matter so much.\n\nEven trying to salvage the experience by playing with bots wasn't much better. They frequently just stand still, get stuck, or seem to be able to immediately land headshots the moment they see you. So there really isn't a reasonable substitute for playing against actual people.\n\nThe declining playerbase wasn't the only thing that hurt the game, either. The microtransactions and the market economy eventually became increasingly difficult to ignore. The user interface aggressively pushes the container mechanics: you're constantly reminded about crates you've unlocked, crates you could buy, and the market itself.\n\nIs this World of Tanks again, where it tries to shove microtransactions down my throat at every turn? It's not even a P2W game, so why are you pretending to be one?? The monetization is incredibly aggressive, but at the end of the day, a genuinely skilled player with a basic rifle and proper positioning can easily beat someone who spent a fortune on crates but has no idea wtf they're doing. The gunplay is heavily skill based, so why let the greedy UI ruin a good game?\n\nNah, **** that.\n\nThe 2D Counter-Strike concept was genuinely refreshing, and I still think the stealth mechanic alone makes Brain/Out worth experiencing. The huge variety of weapons and different game modes also gave it considerably more depth than I initially expected.\n\nBut none of that matters very much when there simply isn't an active playerbase left to sustain the experience.\n\nI'm glad I played Brain/Out when it was more alive and populated, because I genuinely enjoyed it at the time. Unfortunately, coming back to it now mostly feels like visiting a place that used to be full of people and finding nobody there. A ghost town. It's just a shell of its former self now.\n\nIn conclusion, the game has a great concept. Great weapon variety. Some genuinely unique gameplay ideas. But the playerbase is basically gone, and the bots aren't good enough to replace it. I would not recommend this at all in 2026.",
